+# Summary
+
+**Changed**
+- `evlog/cli.py`: added `evlog report --since DATE` (ISO date or datetime). Only events at or after it are counted. Timestamps without an offset, on either side, are treated as UTC. A bad `--since` value or an unreadable file gives a one-line argparse error (exit 2), not a traceback.
+- `tests/test_report.py`: the teammate's existing test is unchanged. I added tests for a `--since` datetime with an offset, an invalid `--since`, and a missing file.
+
+**Verified**
+- `python -m unittest -v`: 5 tests, all pass, including the teammate's `test_since_excludes_older_days`.
+- `python -m evlog report README.md --since x` printed a clean usage error with no traceback.
+
+**Not verified / unsure**
+- The UTC assumption for offset-less timestamps is mine. Ops may expect local time.
+- Malformed JSON lines or events missing fields in the input file still raise a traceback. I left that alone as out of scope.
+- I didn't update the README.
+- Two of my shell calls were blocked by the workspace guard (one used a path outside the workspace). The first was rerun with a workspace path. The second was my attempt to write this file with a shell heredoc, so I used the Write tool instead.
